--- SingleStepTests 8088 (MIT) ---------------------------------------------------

https://github.com/SingleStepTests/8088 - per-clock recordings of a real 8088,
used to verify the CPU core (tools/tests/cpu-sst.mjs). The test data is not part
of this repository; no file from it was copied, so it is listed here out of
thoroughness, not license obligation.

--- TeX Gyre Schola (GUST Font Licence) -----------------------------------------

The typeface used in the page header — fonts/century-schoolbook-italic.*. A
free clone of Century Schoolbook (Morris Fuller Benton, 1919) by the GUST
e-foundry. The GUST Font Licence is legally identical to the LaTeX Project
Public License, version 1.3c or later; full text in
fonts/GUST-FONT-LICENSE.txt and at
https://www.gust.org.pl/fonts/licenses/GUST-FONT-LICENSE.txt

--- PofoManager (Apache-2.0) -----------------------------------------------------

https://github.com/codaris/PofoManager

Used solely as documentation for the parallel-port file transfer protocol
(framing, checksums, command codes). No code or file from this project was
copied, so no derivative work resulted — it's listed here out of
thoroughness, not license obligation.


===============================================================================
WHAT'S NOT PART OF THIS PROJECT
===============================================================================

rom/        The ROM files and DIP-DOS are property of Atari Corp. and DIP
            Ltd. They are not distributed with this project — you must
            supply your own dump. The emulator accepts one through the
            browser and keeps it in local storage; the files are read on
            your machine and are never uploaded anywhere. Neither this
            project nor a deployment of it redistributes them.

software/   Third-party programs used for testing, each under its own
cards/      author's terms. Not covered by this license.

gfx/        The case artwork is the project author's own work and covered
            by the license above. It depicts Atari hardware, though — the
            Atari names and trademarks belong to their owners and are used
            here purely descriptively.
